@group(:concurrency)
mod Std.Otp.CascadeExit
  ## CASCADING exit propagation across a link chain (build-out map G3 remainder; the liveness
  ## fan-out `Std.Otp.ExitSignal` sets up per-peer but does not iterate).
  ##
  ## When a process dies abnormally, its exit signal propagates to each linked peer per
  ## `Std.Otp.ExitSignal`: a non-trapping peer dies (and its OWN links then propagate), a
  ## trapping peer absorbs the signal as a message and STOPS the cascade. Along a chain of
  ## linked processes this is a CASCADE: the abnormal exit kills every non-trapping process in
  ## turn until it reaches the first trapping process, which absorbs it — everything from that
  ## trapper onward survives. No published type system models this fan-out.
  ##
  ## `Cascade(before, after)` is the propagation relation: driving an abnormal exit through the
  ## chain `before` leaves `after` alive. The key result is `run_cascade`: the cascade is
  ## TOTAL — every chain has a determinate cascade outcome (it always terminates; the survivor
  ## set is exactly the suffix from the first trapping process, or empty if none trap). This is
  ## the liveness guarantee: a cascading exit never runs forever and its reach is decidable
  ## from the trap flags alone.
  ##
  ## **Honest scope.** Links form a CHAIN (a general link graph is a fan-out over this shape);
  ## a process is modelled by its `trap_exit` flag (mailbox/ether abstracted — the trapping
  ## process's `EXIT`-acceptance is the `Std.Otp.ExitSignal` obligation, discharged there);
  ## death is terminal. Types the propagation STRUCTURE: termination + the trapper cutoff.

  type Trap = Trapping | NotTrapping
  ## A chain of linked processes, by trap flag, in propagation order.
  type Chain = CNil | CCons(Trap, Chain)

  ## The cascade relation: an abnormal exit driven into `before` leaves `after` alive.
  ##   CascEmpty — an empty chain: nothing to propagate;
  ##   CascStop  — the head traps: it absorbs the exit as a message, the whole chain survives;
  ##   CascProp  — the head does not trap: it dies, and the exit propagates into the rest.
  type Cascade indices (before: Chain, after: Chain)
    CascEmpty : Cascade(CNil, CNil)
    CascStop  : Cascade(CCons(Trapping, rest), CCons(Trapping, rest))
    CascProp  : Cascade(rest, after) -> Cascade(CCons(NotTrapping, rest), after)

  ## A chain paired with its cascade outcome — the existential "there is an `after`".
  type CascadeOf indices (before: Chain)
    MkCascadeOf : (after: Chain) -> Cascade(before, after) -> CascadeOf(before)

  ## **LIVENESS: the cascade is TOTAL.** Every chain has a determinate cascade outcome — the
  ## propagation always terminates, and the survivors are computed by scanning to the first
  ## trapping process. Match the chain, then its head's trap flag: a trapper stops the cascade
  ## (survivors = the whole suffix); a non-trapper dies and the cascade recurses on the rest.
  fn run_cascade(c: Chain) -> CascadeOf(c) = match c
    CNil()      -> MkCascadeOf(CNil, CascEmpty())
    CCons(t, r) -> match t
      Trapping    -> MkCascadeOf(CCons(Trapping, r), CascStop())
      NotTrapping -> match run_cascade(r)
        MkCascadeOf(after, casc) -> MkCascadeOf(after, CascProp(casc))
end
